# Hosting the browser app on Cloud Run

The browser app can run on Google Cloud Run, behind Google sign-in (IAP) for the accounts you name. The commands,
and any app that is not the browser app, stay on your computer. This repo's deployment: project `{{ gcp_project }}`
in `{{ gcp_region }}`, service `{{ project_name }}`.

| Setting | Value |
|---|---|
| Project | `{{ gcp_project }}` |
| Region | `{{ gcp_region }}` |
| Service | `{{ project_name }}` |
| Service account | `{{ project_name }}-app@{{ gcp_project }}.iam.gserviceaccount.com` |
| Secret | `motherduck-token` |
| Data | `md:{{ motherduck_db }}`, the copy that `/publish` sends |

## The architecture

```mermaid
flowchart LR
    viewer([Browser]) -->|1. every request| iap[IAP<br/>is this person allowed?]
    iap -->|not signed in| auth[Google sign-in<br/>Google Auth Platform]
    auth -->|signed in| iap
    iap -->|2. allowed| run[Cloud Run<br/>the Streamlit app]
    secret[(Secret Manager<br/>MotherDuck token)] -.->|at start| run
    run -->|3. first session, Reload data| md[(MotherDuck<br/>md:{{ motherduck_db }})]
    computer[Your computer] -->|/publish| md
    computer -->|gcloud run deploy| build[Cloud Build] -->|image| registry[(Artifact Registry)] -->|runs| run
```

### Opening the app

**IAP (Identity-Aware Proxy) controls who gets in.** Only IAP's own account can call the app
(`roles/run.invoker`). A browser with no IAP cookie goes to Google sign-in. Then IAP admits only the accounts in
its list (`roles/iap.httpsResourceAccessor`, see *Sharing it with someone*).

**Google Auth Platform supplies the sign-in page** (OAuth), in three parts that you make in the console (step 7):
*Branding* (app name and contact email), *Audience* (**External** and published, so any Google account can sign
in) and an *OAuth client* (IAP's credentials to ask Google who the person is).

```mermaid
sequenceDiagram
    participant B as Browser
    participant I as IAP
    participant G as Google sign-in
    participant A as App (Cloud Run)
    B->>I: open the app
    I->>B: no IAP cookie: go to Google sign-in
    B->>G: sign in (name and email only)
    G->>I: this is you@gmail.com (via the OAuth client)
    I->>I: on the allowed list?
    I->>B: yes: set the IAP cookie
    B->>I: open the app, with the cookie
    I->>A: forward the request
    A->>B: the page, then a live connection (WebSocket)
```

**Cloud Run runs the app.**

- **Instances.** With no visitors, no instance runs and nothing is billed (`--min-instances=0`). The first visit
  starts one in a few seconds (a *cold start*). `--max-instances=1`: all viewers share one instance.
- **The live connection.** A Streamlit page keeps a WebSocket open (`/_stcore/stream`). Cloud Run counts it as one
  long request, so **an open tab keeps the instance running and billed**, even with no clicks. `--timeout=3600`
  closes it after one hour, and the page connects again.
- **Sessions.** Each tab is a session with its own `st.session_state`. A reload, or a new instance, starts a new
  session.
- **The data.** The first session copies the contract views from MotherDuck into memory, shared by all viewers
  (`st.cache_resource`). *Reload data* replaces the copy for all viewers.

**The idle pause.** After 15 minutes with no pointer, key, scroll or touch event, a tab goes to a static *Paused*
page with no live connection, so the instance can stop. *Resume* loads the same page again. duckkit's
`app.common.watch_idle` does this when `{{ env_prefix }}_APP_DB` is set (a hosted app). `{{ env_prefix }}_IDLE_MINUTES`
changes the delay (`--update-env-vars` on the deploy), and `0` disables the idle pause.

**Secret Manager holds the MotherDuck token**, a read-only token (step 4). The secret `motherduck-token` keeps
the token out of the code and the image. The service account `{{ project_name }}-app` can only read that secret.
At each instance start, Cloud Run reads it as that account into `MOTHERDUCK_TOKEN` (`--set-secrets`), which
overrides `[motherduck] token` in config.toml.

**MotherDuck holds the data**: the copy that `/publish` sends. The app attaches it `READ_ONLY` (as it does every
source), but that guards only against the app's own code: a read-write token in the secret could still write to
every database of the MotherDuck account. Google Cloud stores none of the app's data: only the viewers' email
addresses (IAP) and the request logs.

### Deploying new code

```mermaid
flowchart LR
    src[The repo<br/>only code: .gcloudignore] -->|gcloud run deploy --source .| build[Cloud Build<br/>runs the Dockerfile]
    build -->|container image| reg[(Artifact Registry<br/>cloud-run-source-deploy)]
    reg -->|new revision| run[Cloud Run]
```

- **Cloud Build** follows the `Dockerfile` to make a *container image*: Python, the packages from `uv.lock`, and
  the MotherDuck extension. `.gcloudignore` uploads only the code. `.dockerignore` lets only the code into the
  image: never `data/` or `config.toml`.
- **Artifact Registry** stores the images (about 0.4 GB each). Cloud Run needs the serving revision's image at
  each cold start. A *cleanup policy* keeps the 2 most recent images (serving, and one for a rollback) and deletes
  older ones about once a day.
- **Cloud Run** starts the new image as a new *revision* and sends all traffic to it. The URL stays the same.

### The account around it

- **The project** `{{ gcp_project }}` holds all of the above.
- **The billing account** pays, with a card. Google requires one, even inside the free tier.
- **The budget alert** sends an email at 50% and 100% of a small monthly amount. It stops nothing.
- **A spend cap** (optional, console only): *Billing → Budgets & alerts → Create budget → Spend cap enforcement*,
  for this project and Cloud Run. Above the cap, Google pauses Cloud Run (nothing deleted) until the next month.
- **APIs** on: the ones in step 2. They cost nothing until used.

## Costs

| Item | Expected cost |
|---|---|
| Cloud Run (1 vCPU, 1 GiB, 0–1 instances) | nothing for personal use: the free tier is about 50 hours a month of one running instance |
| Artifact Registry (2 images kept, about 0.4 GB each) | a few cents a month (0.5 GB free) |
| Cloud Build | nothing (free daily build minutes) |
| Secret Manager (one secret) | nothing |
| MotherDuck | free on the free plan, with a read-write token (a deviation, step 4). The read-scaling token of step 4 needs a paid plan |

The only cost that grows is an instance that an open tab keeps running: the idle pause limits it to 15 minutes,
and with one instance, many tabs cost the same as one.

## One-time setup

Replace `ME` with your Google account, `BILLING_ACCOUNT_ID` with your billing account, `5USD` with a budget in
your currency, `CLIENT_ID` and `client_secret_….json` with your OAuth client's ID and file (step 7),
`THEIR_EMAIL` with the account of a person you share the app with, and `PREVIOUS_REVISION` with a revision's name
(*Updating*).

1. **Install the gcloud CLI and sign in.**
   ```sh
   brew install --cask google-cloud-sdk
   gcloud auth login
   ```
2. **Create the project and link billing.** Cloud Run needs a billing account, even inside the free tier. Create
   the billing account in the console (it asks for a card). Then:
   ```sh
   gcloud projects create {{ gcp_project }}
   gcloud billing accounts list
   gcloud billing projects link {{ gcp_project }} --billing-account=BILLING_ACCOUNT_ID
   gcloud services enable --project={{ gcp_project }} run.googleapis.com cloudbuild.googleapis.com \
       artifactregistry.googleapis.com \
       secretmanager.googleapis.com iap.googleapis.com billingbudgets.googleapis.com \
       cloudresourcemanager.googleapis.com
   gcloud artifacts repositories create cloud-run-source-deploy --repository-format=docker \
       --location={{ gcp_region }} --project={{ gcp_project }}
   cat > cleanup.json <<'EOF'
   [{"name": "keep-2-most-recent", "action": {"type": "Keep"}, "mostRecentVersions": {"keepCount": 2}},
    {"name": "delete-older", "action": {"type": "Delete"}, "condition": {"tagState": "any"}}]
   EOF
   gcloud artifacts repositories set-cleanup-policies cloud-run-source-deploy --location={{ gcp_region }} \
       --project={{ gcp_project }} --policy=cleanup.json --no-dry-run
   rm cleanup.json
   ```
   A *Keep* rule always wins over a *Delete* rule.
3. **Make a budget alert** (an email at 50% and 100%):
   ```sh
   gcloud billing budgets create --billing-account=BILLING_ACCOUNT_ID --display-name={{ project_name }} \
       --budget-amount=5USD --threshold-rule=percent=0.5 --threshold-rule=percent=1.0
   ```
4. **Store a read-only MotherDuck token as a secret.** Make a read-only token in MotherDuck's settings (a *read
   scaling* token), so the hosted app cannot change your data. Paste it at the prompt: the screen does not show it,
   and an empty paste stops the command before gcloud runs.
   ```sh
   read -rs TOKEN && [ -n "$TOKEN" ] && printf %s "$TOKEN" |
       gcloud secrets create motherduck-token --replication-policy=user-managed --locations={{ gcp_region }} \
       --project={{ gcp_project }} --data-file=-; unset TOKEN
   ```
   Read-scaling tokens need a paid MotherDuck plan. Without one, the hosted app would need the read-write token that
   `publish` uses. The scaffold promises a read-only token ({{ scaffold_repo }}, docs/architecture.md), so that is a
   deviation of this app: record it in docs/deviations.md. A read-scaling token reads replicas that take up to a
   minute to see a new `publish`.
5. **Make a service account for the app.** It can read that one secret, and nothing else:
   ```sh
   gcloud iam service-accounts create {{ project_name }}-app --project={{ gcp_project }}
   gcloud secrets add-iam-policy-binding motherduck-token --project={{ gcp_project }} \
       --member=serviceAccount:{{ project_name }}-app@{{ gcp_project }}.iam.gserviceaccount.com \
       --role=roles/secretmanager.secretAccessor
   ```
6. **Build and deploy** from the repo root, with the command of `/hosting deploy` (the skill holds the canonical
   flags). Cloud Build builds the `Dockerfile`:
   ```sh
   gcloud run deploy {{ project_name }} --source . --project={{ gcp_project }} --region={{ gcp_region }} \
       --service-account={{ project_name }}-app@{{ gcp_project }}.iam.gserviceaccount.com \
       --set-secrets=MOTHERDUCK_TOKEN=motherduck-token:latest \
       --update-labels=commit=$(git rev-parse --short HEAD) \
       --update-env-vars={{ env_prefix }}_VERSION=$(git rev-parse --short HEAD) \
       --no-allow-unauthenticated --iap \
       --min-instances=0 --max-instances=1 --cpu=1 --memory=1Gi --timeout=3600 --session-affinity
   ```
   The label and the variable record the commit: `status --remote`, `/hosting status` and `/catch-up` compare the
   label with `origin/main`, and the sidebar shows the variable (*version …*).
7. **Enable sign-in (IAP).** A project with no Google organisation needs its own OAuth client. Google's built-in
   client admits only the users of an organisation (without one, the app shows *Empty Google Account OAuth
   client ID(s)/secret(s)*). gcloud cannot create the client, so start in the console:
   - *Google Auth Platform → Branding*: an app name and your email. *Audience*: **External**, then *Publish app*.
     Sign-in asks only for name and email, so Google does not review the app. If you keep it in *Testing*, add
     each viewer as a test user.
   - *Google Auth Platform → Clients → Create client*: **Web application**. *Download JSON* (the
     `client_secret_….json` file). Then edit the client and add the authorised redirect URI
     `https://iap.googleapis.com/v1/oauth/clientIds/CLIENT_ID:handleRedirect`, with its client ID.
   - Attach the client to the service from the downloaded file, so the screen never shows the secret:
     ```sh
     python3 - client_secret_….json > iap-oauth.yaml <<'EOF'
     import json, sys
     web = json.load(open(sys.argv[1]))["web"]
{%- raw %}
     print(f"accessSettings:\n  oauthSettings:\n    clientId: {web['client_id']}\n    clientSecret: {web['client_secret']}")
{%- endraw %}
     EOF
     gcloud iap settings set iap-oauth.yaml --resource-type=cloud-run --region={{ gcp_region }} --service={{ project_name }} \
         --project={{ gcp_project }}
     rm iap-oauth.yaml client_secret_….json
     ```

   Then let IAP call the service, and let yourself in:
   ```sh
   PROJECT_NUMBER=$(gcloud projects describe {{ gcp_project }} --format='value(projectNumber)')
   gcloud run services add-iam-policy-binding {{ project_name }} --project={{ gcp_project }} --region={{ gcp_region }} \
       --member=serviceAccount:service-$PROJECT_NUMBER@gcp-sa-iap.iam.gserviceaccount.com --role=roles/run.invoker
   gcloud iap web add-iam-policy-binding --resource-type=cloud-run --service={{ project_name }} \
       --region={{ gcp_region }} --project={{ gcp_project }} --member=user:ME --role=roles/iap.httpsResourceAccessor
   ```
8. **Open it:** `gcloud run services describe {{ project_name }} --project={{ gcp_project }} --region={{ gcp_region }} --format='value(status.url)'`. Make sure that the
   same command without `--format` shows `Iap Enabled: true`, and that a private window asks you to sign in.

## Updating

`/hosting` in Claude Code deploys, rolls back, adds or removes a person, lists who has access, and shows the
status.

- **New data:** `/publish`, then *Reload data* (with a read-scaling token, wait a minute first).
  `uv run {{ project_name }} publish --check` tells you if MotherDuck has the latest data.
- **New code:** after a merge, `/hosting deploy` (or step 6 from `main`). `status --remote` and `/hosting status`
  compare the deployed commit with `origin/main`.
- **Roll back** with no new build: `/hosting rollback`. By hand, find the previous revision's name, then send all
  traffic to it:
  ```sh
  gcloud run revisions list --service={{ project_name }} --project={{ gcp_project }} --region={{ gcp_region }}
  gcloud run services update-traffic {{ project_name }} --project={{ gcp_project }} --region={{ gcp_region }} \
      --to-revisions=PREVIOUS_REVISION=100
  ```
  The cleanup policy keeps the 2 most recent images, so a rollback is safe only when the newest revision serves and
  the second-newest is Ready. In any other case (after a rollback, or after a failed deploy), deploy a fixed commit
  instead. If a deploy after a
  rollback does not serve 100 percent, send the traffic to it:
  `gcloud run services update-traffic {{ project_name }} --project={{ gcp_project }} --region={{ gcp_region }} --to-latest`.
- **A new MotherDuck token:** store it as a new version of the secret, then deploy again:
  ```sh
  read -rs TOKEN && [ -n "$TOKEN" ] && printf %s "$TOKEN" |
      gcloud secrets versions add motherduck-token --project={{ gcp_project }} --data-file=-; unset TOKEN
  ```

## Sharing it with someone

You can add any Google account (Gmail, or an address registered with Google). Only the accounts you add can open the
app, and they see all the data that `/publish` sends. Access can take a few minutes to start.

```sh
gcloud iap web add-iam-policy-binding --resource-type=cloud-run --service={{ project_name }} \
    --region={{ gcp_region }} --project={{ gcp_project }} --member=user:THEIR_EMAIL --role=roles/iap.httpsResourceAccessor
```

- **Remove a person:** run the same command with `remove-iam-policy-binding`. A tab that the person has open stops
  at its next reconnect: after the idle pause, or after one hour at most (`--timeout=3600`).
- **See who has access:** `gcloud iap web get-iam-policy --resource-type=cloud-run --service={{ project_name }}
  --region={{ gcp_region }} --project={{ gcp_project }}`.
- **Testing mode:** if the OAuth app is still in *Testing* (step 7), also add the person as a test user.

## One hosted app for each project

A project hosts one app: this app's browser app. Its other Streamlit apps (`extra_apps`, a curation tool, say) run
on your computer only: the image runs the browser app alone, even for an extra app with `hosted: true` (which only
means that it reads only the contract). Another app of this kind gets its own project, with its own answers to
`gcp_project` and `gcp_region`.

## Deleting it

`gcloud run services delete {{ project_name }} --project={{ gcp_project }} --region={{ gcp_region }}` stops the app. `gcloud projects delete {{ gcp_project }}` deletes
all of it, including the secret and the images.
